In article <3607@milton.acs.washington.edu> mtv@milton.u.washington.edu (David Schanen) writes:
>
>   First of all players can't create save files, I'm assuming it's because of
>the suid situation.

I don't know what your problem is.  Save files do work, and they don't
depend on the suid code.  However, it could be some side effect of you
defining SECURE when you should not have defined it, try recompiling without
SECURE.

>Second there was no option for SECURE without those 'ANDREW' libs, whatever
>those are.  I got around this simply by commenting out the '#ifdef SECURE' 
>and 'else shell_out' stuff forcing the refusal message.  I don't undertand wh
>people build these blatant security holes into they're code. If you really wa
>to give shell access, it can't be very hard to seteuid back to uid can it?

There are no blatant security holes in the code.  The first thing the
program does is grab a file pointer to the score file, and then it
immediately relinquishes SUID priviledges.  Nothing else is done while
the program is SUID.  The game is reasonably secure, and games should not
be SUID root anyways so there shouldn't be any problems with umoria.

The SECURE option only supports ANDREW because that is the only system that
needs it.  ANDREW is a distributed file system, and setuid apparently does
not work the same way, hence the need for special code to make moria secure
under ANDREW.  I say 'apparently', because I've never used ANDREW myself;
the special ANDREW code was contributed by a CMU student where ANDREW
was developed.

>   Thirdly I get 'scoring not implemented yet' with moria -s , is this true?

That's what the message says, doesn't it?

>Will we have scoring in the near future?  I'm not sure how the masses will
>take this, it may turn them off to the game before they even get started.  Do
>you think I should wait until scoring is implemented?

The lack of scoring is a historical accident, as they say.  It was going
to be integrated with the savefile protection code, but when the concept
of savefile protection was scrapped, the scoring code had to be scrapped
also.  Since then, I haven't done any work on scoring, preferring instead
to work on more interesting things like portability, bugs, and new features.

Moria was posted without scoring because I was getting more requests for
posting the sources than complaints about the lack of scoring.  No doubt
this situation will change very soon.

>   And what about this 'wizard mode for everyone', I just don't know.

The last version, umoria 4.87, has 'wizard mode for everyone' also, it just
wasn't as obvious.  All you had to do was ftp the sources and recompile it
with yourself defined as the wizard, or else play one of the microcomputer
versions.  The new version just makes it clear that any one can be wizard.

The new version keeps much better track of the use of wizard mode so
that once a character has been in wizard mode, that character will never
be added to the scoreboard (assuming there is one of course).  So it
is reasonably safe to allow anyone use of wizard mode.  Yes, it is
possible for people to abuse wizard mode, but they could have done the
same things in umoria 4.87 also.  If someone wants to cheat, there is
nothing I can do that will stop it.

I could also say that the new version will save disk space.  I suspect
that many large universities have 10 or more copies of moria in their
filesystems, one for each student that likes to use wizard mode.  In
the new version, one copy of the game will be sufficient.

If you want to start a discussion about features/misfeatures of umoria,
please send followups to rec.games.moria where this discussion belongs.
